  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread frost is expected for northern areas\non Saturday night, with patchy frost elsewhere in interior \nDowneast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n1030mb sfc high builds in across the north on Saturday. Clear \nskies and light winds will result in excellent radn/l cooling \nconds. Temps drop to around 30 in the north, with deeper valleys\ndipping into the low-mid 20s by dawn. Widespread frost is expected\nto the north of Bangor. \n\nPatchy to areas of frost may occur in the Bangor Region late \nSaturday night. Cannot rule out frost for interior Downeast and \nalong the coast, especially if the high can build in faster than \ncurrently advertised. People should be prepared to protect any \nvulnerable vegetation across the area, especially if they are in \nlower lying areas that typically gets colder. \n\nThe frost/freeze program has ended for all counties except Hancock \nand Washington counties, so frost headlines would not be issued for \nPenobscot, Piscataquis, northern Somerset, or Aroostook counties. \nCoastal Downeast should stay warm enough to limit any frost, though \ncheck the forecast for updates. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain is possible Monday \nfollowed by gusty westerly winds on Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSunday night, a cold surface high pressure that will have \nbrought widespread frost Sunday AM shifts to the east over the \nMaritimes. A potent vertically stacked low pressure system \ntracks into north-central Quebec with a trailing potent cold \nfront. That front will swing across the CWA on Monday at the \nsame time as a low pressure exits off the Mid-Atlantic coast. \nThe interaction between the high pressure and the two lows \nprovides significant uncertainty about rainfall. There a few \ndifferent scenarios still with the models. 1. The high pressure \ncould dominate, forcing the lows to stay to the north and \nsouth, limiting precip. 2. The Canadian low could enhance the \ncoastal low as it rides the coast north, bringing more \nsubstantial precip to coastal areas. 3. The Canadian low could \ndominate, bringing enhanced precipitation along its cold front \nfrom the moisture over the southeast low. At this time, \nuncertainty from the models is high, so forecast confidence is \nlow, with a wide range of precipitation values. \n\nIncreasing confidence on Tuesday, as the low pressure system \ndeepens in the Northern Maritimes, we can expect strong surface \nhigh pressure to dive over the eastern Great Lakes. This will \nlead to a strong pressure gradient between the low and high \nresulting in gusty west winds developing during the afternoon \nand evening hours on Monday. Tuesday probabilities for 35+ mph \nwind gusts have increased to 70-90% with 40+ mph probabilities \nnow 50-70%, mainly for Millinocket and areas north. There is \nensemble guidance suggesting a potential of wind headlines but \nstill uncertainty exists on exact expected wind speeds/gusts. \nHowever, it is likely that significant leaf loss may be possible\nin areas of Northern Maine where peak foliage has been reached.\nAdditional leaf loss possible in the Central Highlands to \nDowneast Maine where the foliage is moderate to high. Continue \nto monitor the forecast for updates, as models begin to get a \nbetter handle on the atmospheric set up.",
